        "scope": "project",
        "content": "# Project context\n\n## Active task\nWrite an approximately 1,000-character practical accessibility-review case in Chinese, directly in the originating conversation. The user now requests a third-round continuation, superseding the earlier two-round scenario. They explicitly request no further webpage reading and no memory or file saving as part of the requested answer.\n\nReuse the two previously used sources:\n- W3C WAI, Introduction to Web Accessibility: https://www.w3.org/WAI/fundamentals/accessibility-intro/\n- MDN, Accessibility: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility\n\n### Required scenario\nFollow three inputs in the same conversation:\n1. The first two rounds have ended while the third continues generating.\n2. All three rounds subsequently end.\n3. The physical standby session is released.\n4. The user reopens the historical conversation.\n\nExplain concretely how an accessibility reviewer determines:\n- Which particular input was genuinely canceled, rather than merely being an already-ended historical record.\n- How queued messages are retained and remain identifiable.\n- Whether each source's title, domain, and locator still belong to the correct input.\n- Whether each input's identity, runtime status, and associated body content remain correctly linked throughout the transitions.\n\nThe user asks how to establish these distinctions; the evidence does not specify that a particular round was actually canceled or define the product's queue/cancellation behavior. A hypothetical test sequence may be used, but must not present assumed behavior as observed fact or as a standard mandated by the sources.\n\nContinue distinguishing three independent facts: the answer has ended, the physical session remains on standby, and background tasks still have work to do. Releasing the physical session or reopening history must not be conflated with cancellation of an input. Retain source citations and distinguish source-supported accessibility principles from proposed product behavior.\n\n## Completed work and continuity\nTwo Chinese responses have been delivered:\n- A general guide, “会话型产品无障碍审查指南,” covering source lists, streaming content, per-input status, keyboard navigation, and reopening history. The original approximately 1,500-character general-guide request was superseded by practical-case requests.\n- A practical case, “实际审查案例：两轮问答与历史恢复,” addressing the first round ending, the second generating, reviewing old sources, switching conversations, and reopening history. This fulfilled the preceding two-round request; the current task extends it to three inputs, explicit cancellation/queue distinctions, physical-session release, and source attribution details.\n\nThe delivered two-round case used a research-assistant example: first input compares two reports; second supplements risk analysis. It explicitly labeled its input-identification, state-association, and history-restoration proposals as product recommendations, not runtime models prescribed by W3C or MDN.\n\nNo response to the latest third-round request is recorded. There are no attachments.\n\n## Established review principles to reuse\n- Give each input a stable identity, understandable label, input text, and conversation association. Bind body content, runtime status, source references, and background work to that input rather than relying only on list position, timestamps, or a global spinner.\n- Distinguish queued, processing, completed, stopped/canceled, and failed states where supported. Body generation ending does not establish that all background work has ended. Do not infer physical-session liveness or task status from presentation alone; state uncertainty where the underlying status cannot be confirmed.\n- Stop and retry controls need clear scope. A retry should have a distinguishable execution and should not silently overwrite earlier results. Switching conversations is not evidence of cancellation; reading an old input's sources does not change the execution target.\n- A late source-check result from an older round must update that round's source records, not the newest round's body or sources. Opening an older citation must not silently switch the source panel to the latest input.\n- Sources need identifiable titles and destinations and traceability from the relevant body citations. Merely listing a URL does not prove successful reading or evidential support. Distinguish discovered, successfully read, failed, and supporting sources. The latest request adds explicit checking of title, domain, and locator ownership across rounds and history reopening.\n- Keep body reading separate from runtime announcements. Streaming updates should not steal focus, force scrolling away from older text, or default to character-by-character announcements. Offer a way to reach new content and return to the prior citation/reading position.\n- Test the complete workflow with keyboard and assistive technology, including sources, stop/retry, conversation switching, and historical reopening. Do not broadcast another conversation's entire body as a status notification.\n- Preserve inputs, bodies, references, and final statuses in history. Historical reopening should not automatically replay all text, retry requests, or treat an unverified old “processing” label as a current live state. Interrupted/incomplete results should remain identifiable. A new session displaying history does not prove that the original physical session remained alive.\n- The prior case's acceptance principle was that every status should identify which input it belongs to, which object it describes, and what evidence supports it. Apply this especially to cancellation versus historical completion, queued-message retention, and session release.\n\n## Source basis and evidence limits\nPrior responses attributed the following principles to the sources:\n- W3C: accessibility supports perception, understanding, navigation, interaction, and contribution; keyboard access matters; knowledgeable human evaluation is needed in addition to automated checks.\n- MDN: use semantic HTML and appropriate ARIA to support complex controls and dynamic content.\n\nThe prior guide explicitly noted that the MDN overview does not prescribe streaming-announcement frequency and that the proposed checklist alone does not establish WCAG conformance. The practical case similarly separated broad accessibility principles from product-specific runtime and history recommendations.\n\nThe available evidence includes the delivered responses but no separate browsing/tool record or source excerpts. These are prior-response attributions, not newly verified findings. The user treats the sources as already read and prohibits reading the webpages again.\n",
